⍝ An event type and a kernel, Arvo's shape at small scale
⍝ (RUNTIME-DESIGN.md section 3b): the runtime builds an event datum from
⍝ the program's own constructors, applies poke to the state and the
⍝ event, installs the new state, and emits the effects.  Nothing is
⍝ reified; the runtime constructs data and applies.
nat ≡ Zero | Suc nat
event ≡ Tick | Poke nat
effect ≡ Log nat
effects ≡ Nil | Cons effect effects

add m n = n ▹ { Zero m; Suc k (Suc (add m k)) }

⍝ poke: (state, event) -> [state' effects].  A core cannot name itself
⍝ in its own equations, so the new kernel is the new state and the
⍝ runtime re-applies poke to it.
kernel st ≔ {
  poke ev = ev ▹ { Tick [(Suc st) Nil]; Poke n [(add st n) (Cons (Log st) Nil)] }
}
